1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:04,960
Module five, video five point two. Nothing generated is edited by hand.

2
00:00:06,420 --> 00:00:10,920
The evening before the minister's demonstration, someone notices a wrong label on a form.

3
00:00:11,860 --> 00:00:12,920
What is the quickest fix?

4
00:00:13,960 --> 00:00:18,840
Well, uh, the instinct is to just jump right into the platform and, you know, fix it manually.

5
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:21,500
It is just so tempting when you are under pressure.

6
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:24,680
Right. Just a quick platform edit to save the demo.

7
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:28,800
But, um, why is that actually so dangerous for a system?

8
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:33,980
Because that hand edit instantly creates two versions of the truth.

9
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:36,320
Two versions, like they get out of sync.

10
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:42,420
Exactly. I mean, the accepted description says one thing, but the live platform now shows another.

11
00:00:42,980 --> 00:00:47,440
And during the next generation, uh, one of two bad things happens.

12
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:49,340
Okay, number one being?

13
00:00:49,960 --> 00:00:54,600
Number one, the edit is overwritten, so that wrong label just returns.

14
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:57,520
Ah, which completely defeats the purpose of the fix.

15
00:00:57,860 --> 00:00:58,140
Right.

16
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:02,660
Or number two, generation is quietly stopped.

17
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:05,640
Wait, they just stop the generation entirely?

18
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:06,240
They do.

19
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:08,760
Just to protect one manual edit.

20
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:12,280
They do. And then nobody knows what the application is built from anymore.

21
00:01:12,680 --> 00:01:16,900
Your whole system becomes, you know, fragmented, siloed, and just really costly to change.

22
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:24,400
So if we can't do a quick fix, uh, what is the actual SDD method for handling this in your ministry?

23
00:01:25,220 --> 00:01:28,600
It is a really strict procedure. Mm. Um, there are three steps.

24
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,920
Step one: find the document that owns the fact.

25
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:34,780
Okay, so go back to the original description?

26
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:35,440
Yes.

27
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:38,940
Step two: correct it there and have it accepted.

28
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:41,660
And step three: generate the application again.

29
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:44,700
Okay, that sounds foolproof in theory,

30
00:01:45,340 --> 00:01:49,880
but I mean, how do you actually catch a supplier who just sneaks in a hand edit anyway?

31
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:52,900
Well, that is where the read back program comes in.

32
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:55,600
It compares the files and gives one of three marks.

33
00:01:56,500 --> 00:01:56,980
And those are?

34
00:01:57,820 --> 00:02:01,740
Mark one is in step. That means everything matches the design perfectly.

35
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:04,040
Mark two is out of date.

36
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:08,220
Meaning, uh, the blueprint changed, but the file wasn't regenerated.

37
00:02:08,820 --> 00:02:09,820
Exactly. Okay.

38
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:13,560
And mark three is the one you really don't want: edited by hand.

39
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:18,860
Ah, caught red-handed. So let's look at the fictional country of Progressa.

40
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:21,260
Say someone changes a label on a

41
00:02:21,920 --> 00:02:24,540
MoEYS form by hand right before a demo.

42
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:27,420
What will the demonstration actually show?

43
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:31,080
The recording will show a label changed by hand on the platform.

44
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:35,520
Then it will show the read back run and naming the form as edited by hand.

45
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:36,900
So it flags it immediately.

46
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:37,320
Right.

47
00:02:38,020 --> 00:02:41,540
Then it will show the correction made in the model and the application generated again.

48
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:43,720
Finally, the read back run once more.

49
00:02:44,360 --> 00:02:46,760
And it only passes when it is completely clear.

50
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:52,660
Yes. It will pass when the first run names the edited file and the second shows every file in step.

51
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:57,260
Wow. Okay, but how do we force suppliers to actually follow this process?

52
00:02:57,760 --> 00:03:00,680
You lock it down. There are three essential contract terms.

53
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:01,500
Term one:

54
00:03:02,140 --> 00:03:03,920
no generated file is edited by hand.

55
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:06,000
Nice and strict. What is the second?

56
00:03:06,540 --> 00:03:07,140
Term two:

57
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:11,380
the read back run before every acceptance and its list handed over.

58
00:03:11,980 --> 00:03:13,880
So the ministry sees the actual proof.

59
00:03:14,220 --> 00:03:19,000
Exactly. And term three: a file edited by hand is an automatic defect.

60
00:03:19,440 --> 00:03:21,760
Correct it in the description. No exceptions.

61
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:31,740
So to recap for anyone managing these programs, always correct the accepted document, never the platform, and rely on the read back program to catch those manual

62
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:33,700
changes. Absolutely. Absolutely.

63
00:03:34,320 --> 00:03:37,140
That is how you stop your platform from being duplicated and rekeyed.

64
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:37,840
So true.

